bugs — emacs-style keybindings for acme
======================================

Architecture
------------

bugs extends acme with configurable keybindings without modifying
acme's source (aside from one small patch). It works by proxying the
devdraw connection — the pipe between acme and its display server.

    OS keyboard → devdraw → [ddproxy.sl filter] → acme
                                  ↓
                             9P commands
                             (via shell)

The StreetLISP filter (ddproxy.sl) inspects devdraw wire protocol
messages. For keyboard responses (Rrdkbd/Rrdkbd4), it either rewrites
the rune (for simple translations like C-b → left arrow) or replaces
it with a NUL no-op and issues 9P commands for complex cursor
operations (like C-n → move cursor down one line).


Why a devdraw proxy?
--------------------

Acme's event file cannot prevent keyboard input from reaching acme.
When a window's event file is open, acme reports keyboard events but
still delivers them to the window. Button 2/3 events can be
intercepted via write-back, but keyboard events cannot.

The devdraw proxy is the only point where keyboard input can be
intercepted upstream of acme without modifying acme itself. Acme
launches whatever binary DEVDRAW points to, so injecting a proxy
requires no code changes.


Why a NUL no-op patch?
----------------------

Acme's keyboard handler has no true no-op rune. Every unrecognized
rune is inserted as text. Rune 0 was previously mapped to U+FFFD
(replacement character).

For complex keybindings (C-n, C-p), the proxy must:
1. Send some rune to unblock acme's keyboard thread
2. Then perform cursor movement via 9P

Without a no-op, any rune sent in step 1 would either insert text or
trigger an unwanted command (like Escape affecting selection state).

The patch (acme-nop.patch) changes rune 0 from inserting U+FFFD to
returning immediately — a one-line change in rowtype().


Why stderr for 9P commands (not direct file access)?
----------------------------------------------------

On Plan 9, acme's filesystem is mounted at /mnt/acme and SL could
access it directly with (file). On plan9port (macOS/Linux), the 9P
filesystem is accessed via the 9p command or 9pfuse (FUSE mount).

9pfuse is unreliable on modern macOS:
- macFUSE switched from kernel extensions to a userspace model
- plan9port's 9pfuse still tries to load a kext via load_fusefs
- Even with macFUSE installed and load_macfuse found, 9pfuse
  segfaults (exit 139) on macOS 15+
- The system extension requires manual approval in System Settings
  and may still not work with plan9port's expectations

Rather than depend on FUSE, ddproxy.sl writes structured commands
to stderr:

    C <id> <cmd>     → write to acme/<id>/ctl
    A <id> <addr>    → write to acme/<id>/addr
    D <id> <data>    → write to acme/<id>/data

The ddproxy shell script reads these and dispatches them via the 9p
command, which speaks the 9P protocol directly over the Unix socket
without needing a filesystem mount.

On Plan 9 (9front), the bugs-mount script is unnecessary and the SL
code could be modified to use (file) directly. The stderr protocol
still works there via the 9p command.


Why StreetLISP?
---------------

The keybinding logic lives in ddproxy.sl so it can be reconfigured
without recompilation. SL provides:
- Binary I/O (io-read/io-write with typed data and endianness)
- Bitwise operations for protocol parsing
- Hash tables for keybinding lookup
- A compact runtime suitable for embedding in a pipeline

The filter is ~150 lines of SL. Adding a new rune translation is
one line in the *bindings* table. Adding a new 9P action requires
a function and a table entry.


Files
-----

bugs            wrapper script — sets DEVDRAW and launches acme
ddproxy         devdraw proxy — pipes through SL, dispatches 9P
ddproxy.sl      StreetLISP keybinding filter
acme-nop.patch  one-line acme patch — NUL rune as no-op
patch-acme      rc script to apply the patch
bugs-mount      optional FUSE mount script (when 9pfuse works)


Keybindings
-----------

C-a     beginning of line     (native acme)
C-b     left                  (rune translation)
C-e     end of line           (native acme)
C-f     right                 (rune translation)
C-n     cursor down one line  (9P command)
C-p     cursor up one line    (9P command)
